WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.640
In 1961, The Observer printed an
appeal called The Forgotten Prisoners.

2
00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:08.470
It spoke for people
jailed for their beliefs.

3
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.040
It founded Amnesty International.

4
00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:14.150
Long before, Peter and
the apostles answered:

5
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:17.170
we must obey God rather than men.

6
00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:22.140
Yet another verse says every soul should
be subject to the higher authorities.

7
00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:24.860
So when the law and your conscience clash,

8
00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:26.160
who should win?

9
00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:29.030
And can religion make
people more tolerant?

10
00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:32.090
In this mission, you'll set
two verses on a balance,

11
00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:35.040
and see where Thomas
Aquinas draws the line.

12
00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:37.850
Then you'll test John Stuart
Mill's harm principle,

13
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:40.750
and weigh what religion
does for tolerance.

14
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.400
Your mission: weigh up when
conscience may defy the law,

15
00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:46.600
and whether religion breeds tolerance.
